SCOPE L01: [each] Each such definition says that the expression settles down as the parameter goes to its end point.
SCOPE L01: [each] 1 Sequences A sequence is a list \((a_1, a_2, a_3, \dots)\) with one real number \(a_n\) for each positive integer \(n\).
SCOPE L01: [every] We say that \(L\) is the limit of \((a_n)\), and write \(a_n \to L\), when the following holds: for every \(\varepsilon > 0\) there is a positive integer \(N\) such that \(|a_n - L| convergent .
SCOPE L01: [any] The tolerance \(\varepsilon\) is chosen first, and it may be any positive number.
SCOPE L01: [only] The condition must hold from \(N\) onward, not only at \(N\).
SCOPE L01: [every] The definition asks for a threshold at every tolerance.
SCOPE L01: [every] By Definition 2.1 applied to \(L\), there is \(N_1\) with \(|a_n - L| \[|L - M| = |(L - a_n) + (a_n - M)| \le |a_n - L| + |a_n - M| Since \(\varepsilon > 0\) was arbitrary, \(|L - M|\) is smaller than every positive numb
SCOPE L01: [every] A non-negative number smaller than every positive number is zero.
SCOPE L01: [every] For every \(n \ge N\), we have \(n + 1 > N > 1/\varepsilon\), so \(\dfrac{1}{n+1} Example 2.3 chooses \(N\) from \(\varepsilon\) by a formula.
SCOPE L01: [same] The search finds the same kind of threshold, but it checks finitely many values of \(n\).
SCOPE L01: [every] Every term and every error is an exact rational number, and the printed decimals are rounding of those values.
SCOPE L01: [every] The claim that the inequality holds for every \(n \ge 1000\) follows from the identity \(|a_n - 2| = 1/(n+1)\) in Example 2.3 and not from the search.
SCOPE L01: [any] C The printed values are computed facts about the listed terms, and they are not a finding about any other sequence.
SCOPE L01: [every] Proposition 3.1 (the tail decides the limit) Let \((a_n)\) and \((c_n)\) be sequences of real numbers, and let \(M\) be a positive integer such that \(c_n = a_n\) for every \(n \ge M\).
SCOPE L01: [every] 4 Divergence A sequence \((a_n)\) is bounded when there is a real number \(B\) with \(|a_n| \le B\) for every \(n\).
SCOPE L01: [every] Every term satisfies \(|a_n| \le 1\), so the sequence is bounded.
SCOPE L01: [every] Then there is a real number \(B\) with \(|a_n| \le B\) for every positive integer \(n\).
SCOPE L01: [never] The terms of \((2n+1)/(n+1)\) never equal 2, because \(2n + 1 = 2n + 2\) has no solution.
SCOPE L01: [any] The limit is a value the terms approach, and the definition does not require any term to equal it.
SCOPE L01: [all ] The limit claim for all tolerances and all \(n\) is proved by Example 2.3, and no search over finitely many \(n\) can replace that proof.
SCOPE L01: [each] 6 Misconceptions “A sequence with limit \(L\) must reach \(L\) at some finite step.” Definition 2.1 asks that the terms eventually lie within \(\varepsilon\) of \(L\), for each \(\varepsilon\).
SCOPE L01: [each] Its terms are \(1, \tfrac12, \tfrac13, \dots\), and \(1/n > 0\) for each \(n\), so no term is 0.
SCOPE L01: [every] “A bounded sequence must converge.” Example 4.1 gives the bounded sequence \((-1)^n\), with \(|a_n| \le 1\) for every \(n\), which does not converge.
