SCOPE L02: [each,every] We say that \(f\) is continuous at \(a\) when \(f(x_n) \to f(a)\) for every sequence \((x_n)\) with each \(x_n\) in \(D\) and \(x_n \to a\).
SCOPE L02: [every] The function is continuous on \(D\) when it is continuous at every point of \(D\).
SCOPE L02: [every] If \(x_n \to a\), then \(f(x_n) = x_n \to a = f(a)\) by the definition of the limit, so \(f\) is continuous at every \(a\).
SCOPE L02: [each] By Example 2.1 the identity is continuous at each point, and by Corollary 2.2 applied with \(f = g = \mathrm{id}\), the product \(x \mapsto x \cdot x\) is continuous at each point.
SCOPE L02: [every] So \(x^2\) is continuous at every \(a\), with \(\lim x_n^2 = a^2\) for every sequence \(x_n \to a\).
SCOPE L02: [every] Two divergent sequences can also have a convergent sum or product: Misconception 2 gives the sum, and the product of \((-1)^n\) with itself is 1 for every \(n\).
SCOPE L02: [each,only] “A sum converges only when each of its terms converges.” Take \(a_n = (-1)^n\) and \(b_n = -(-1)^n\).
SCOPE L02: [every] The sum \(a_n + b_n\) equals 0 for every \(n\), so it converges to 0.
